<p class=MsoNormal><b><i><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";
color:black'>Note:</span></i></b><i><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";
color:black'> Beginning with the January 2015 survey, the loan categories
referred to in the questions regarding changes in credit standards and demand for
residential mortgage loans have been revised to reflect the Consumer Financial
Protection Bureau&#8217;s qualified mortgage rules. <o:p></o:p></span></i></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><b><i><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";
color:black'>Questions 16-17</span></i></b><i><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
"Times New Roman";color:black'> ask about seven categories of <b>residential
mortgage loans</b> at your bank: Government-Sponsored Enterprise eligible
(GSE-eligible) residential mortgages, government residential mortgages,
Qualified Mortgage non-jumbo non-GSE-eligible (QM non-jumbo, non-GSE-eligible)
residential mortgages, QM jumbo residential mortgages, non-QM jumbo residential
mortgages, non-QM non-jumbo residential mortgages, and subprime residential
mortgages. <o:p></o:p></span></i></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><i><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";
color:black'>For the purposes of this survey, please use the following
definitions of these loan categories and include first-lien closed-end loans to
purchase homes only. The loan categories have been defined so that every
first-lien closed-end residential mortgage loan used for home purchase fits
into one of the following seven categories: <o:p></o:p></span></i></p>

<ul type=disc>
 <li class=MsoNormal style='color:black;mso-margin-top-alt:auto;mso-margin-bottom-alt:
     auto;mso-list:l1 level1 lfo3;tab-stops:list .5in'><i><span
     style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>The <b>GSE-eligible</b>
     category of residential mortgages includes loans that meet the
     underwriting guidelines, including loan limit amounts, of the GSEs -
     Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac. <o:p></o:p></span></i></li>
 <li class=MsoNormal style='color:black;mso-margin-top-alt:auto;mso-margin-bottom-alt:
     auto;mso-list:l1 level1 lfo3;tab-stops:list .5in'><i><span
     style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>The <b>government</b>
     category of residential mortgages includes loans that are insured by the
     Federal Housing Administration, guaranteed by the Department of Veterans Affairs,
     or originated under government programs, including the U.S. Department of
     Agriculture home loan programs. <o:p></o:p></span></i></li>
 <li class=MsoNormal style='color:black;mso-margin-top-alt:auto;mso-margin-bottom-alt:
     auto;mso-list:l1 level1 lfo3;tab-stops:list .5in'><i><span
     style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>The <b>QM non-jumbo,
     non-GSE-eligible</b> category of residential mortgages includes loans that
     satisfy the standards for a qualified mortgage and have loan balances that
     are below the loan limit amounts set by the GSEs but otherwise do not meet
     the GSE underwriting guidelines. <o:p></o:p></span></i></li>
 <li class=MsoNormal style='color:black;mso-margin-top-alt:auto;mso-margin-bottom-alt:
     auto;mso-list:l1 level1 lfo3;tab-stops:list .5in'><i><span
     style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>The <b>QM jumbo</b>
     category of residential mortgages includes loans that satisfy the
     standards for a qualified mortgage but have loan balances that are above
     the loan limit amount set by the GSEs. <o:p></o:p></span></i></li>
 <li class=MsoNormal style='color:black;mso-margin-top-alt:auto;mso-margin-bottom-alt:
     auto;mso-list:l1 level1 lfo3;tab-stops:list .5in'><i><span
     style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>The <b>non-QM jumbo</b>
     category of residential mortgages includes loans that do not satisfy the
     standards for a qualified mortgage and have loan balances that are above
     the loan limit amount set by the GSEs. <o:p></o:p></span></i></li>
 <li class=MsoNormal style='color:black;mso-margin-top-alt:auto;mso-margin-bottom-alt:
     auto;mso-list:l1 level1 lfo3;tab-stops:list .5in'><i><span
     style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>The <b>non-QM non-jumbo</b>
     category of residential mortgages includes loans that do not satisfy the
     standards for a qualified mortgage and have loan balances that are below
     the loan limit amount set by the GSEs.(Please exclude loans classified by
     your bank as subprime in this category.) <o:p></o:p></span></i></li>
 <li class=MsoNormal style='color:black;mso-margin-top-alt:auto;mso-margin-bottom-alt:
     auto;mso-list:l1 level1 lfo3;tab-stops:list .5in'><i><span
     style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>The <b>subprime</b>
     category of residential mortgages includes loans classified by your bank
     as subprime. This category typically includes loans made to borrowers with
     weakened credit histories that include payment delinquencies, charge-offs,
     judgements, and/or bankruptcies; reduced repayment capacity as measured by
     credit scores or debt-to-income ratios; or incomplete credit histories. <o:p></o:p></span></i></li>
</ul>

<p class=MsoNormal><b><i><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";
color:black'>Question 16 </span></i></b><i><span style='mso-fareast-font-family:
"Times New Roman";color:black'>deals with changes in your bank's credit
standards for loans in each of the seven loan categories over the past three
months. If your bank's credit standards have not changed over the relevant
period, please report them as unchanged even if the standards are either
restrictive or accommodative relative to longer-term norms. If your bank's
credit standards have tightened or eased over the relevant period, please so
report them regardless of how they stand relative to longer-term norms. Also,
please report changes in enforcement of existing standards as changes in
standards. <b>Question 17</b> deals with changes in demand for loans in each of
the seven loan categories over the past three months. </span></i><span
style='mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";color:black'><o:p></o:p></span></p>

